WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Chapter one ended with a pull running through every link of the chain.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.960
Can that pull change a link itself?

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.440
To find out, start where the pull begins, inside the cell.

4
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:20.873
Here, a motor protein called myosin II grips the actin cables and slides them past

5
00:00:20.873 --> 00:00:25.093
one another. So the cell can keep its own chains under load.

6
00:00:26.353 --> 00:00:28.873
That pull reaches every link, down to the matrix.

7
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:33.227
Look closer at the integrin chain's adaptor, talin.

8
00:00:34.487 --> 00:00:38.547
Its long rod contains 13 small bundles of alpha α-helices.

9
00:00:39.827 --> 00:00:41.447
Each bundle is folded up tight.

10
00:00:42.517 --> 00:00:47.937
Some of these bundles hide binding sites for vinculin, the second adaptor you met in

11
00:00:47.937 --> 00:00:53.497
chapter one. While a bundle stays folded, its site stays tucked away inside.

12
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:59.470
Now take a purified piece of the talin rod, outside any cell, and stretch it.

13
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.090
A test piece from vinculin waits beside it.

14
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.710
What happens to those hidden sites?

15
00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.130
Make a guess.

16
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.737
The bundles unfold, and the hidden sites open.

17
00:01:18.017 --> 00:01:20.097
The vinculin test pieces bind to them.

18
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:25.557
Back in a cell, activated vinculin can add another tie to actin.

19
00:01:26.817 --> 00:01:28.617
So pulling can recruit reinforcements.

20
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.210
Magnetic tweezers pulled a bead tied to a single mouse talin rod.

21
00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:40.570
Bundles unfolded at about 5 to 25 piconewtons, trillionths of a newton.

22
00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.290
They refolded as force fell.

23
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:48.150
A model using those rates suggests the rod can buffer force.

24
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.810
The cadherin chain has a similar trick.

25
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:57.810
Alpha α-Catenin has a hidden vinculin site of its own, and tension opens it.

26
00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.630
So both chains can reinforce themselves under load.

27
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.380
But is force the only key?

28
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.700
Talin and vinculin each fold shut on their own, hiding their binding sites.

29
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.240
This self-closing is called autoinhibition.

30
00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:17.253
Researchers tested this in mouse fibroblasts.

31
00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:23.773
Normal, autoinhibited talin and vinculin did not bind each other at mitochondria.

32
00:02:25.093 --> 00:02:29.453
Releasing either protein's autoinhibition enabled binding without force.

33
00:02:30.713 --> 00:02:35.953
Those active forms still supported disorganized adhesions when myosin II was

34
00:02:35.953 --> 00:02:36.953
blocked.

35
00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:42.977
So force can expose binding sites, while engineered release from autoinhibition can

36
00:02:42.977 --> 00:02:49.297
bypass force. Whether a cell's own signals do that at the same steps in a tissue is

37
00:02:49.297 --> 00:02:50.297
not known.

38
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.200
And a few piconewtons on one molecule are not the pull of a whole cell.

39
00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.860
One more link, where the catenins meet actin, behaves oddly under force.

40
00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.580
The text picks that up next.

41
00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:06.753
So a chain is more than fixed parts.

42
00:03:07.973 --> 00:03:11.373
Some links do not just hold or break; they respond to pull.

43
00:03:12.713 --> 00:03:14.693
Pulling can open sites and recruit ties.

44
00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:19.333
Engineered release from autoinhibition can bypass force.
